  "affiliation_unit": null,
  "affiliation_detail": "Believed to be controlled by China's Ministry of State Security (MSS). On January 17, 2025, OFAC sanctioned Sichuan Juxinhe Network Technology Co., Ltd. (四川聚信和网络科技有限公司) for \"direct involvement in the Salt Typhoon cyber group.\" \nThe August 2025 CISA multinational advisory linked the activity to at least three Chinese entities: Sichuan Juxinhe, Beijing Huanyu Tianqiong Information Technology Co., Ltd. (北京寰宇天穹信息技 术有限公司), and Sichuan Zhixin Ruijie Network Technology Co., Ltd. (四川智信锐捷网络科技有限公司). According to the CISA advisory, these companies provide cyber products and services to multiple PLA and MSS units.\nNo individuals have been publicly indicted specifically for Salt Typhoon telecom intrusions. However, according to a 2025 SpyCloud report, leaked datasets appearing on DarkForums in 2025 contained non-public documents related to VenusTech (a Chinese IT security vendor serving government clients) and Salt Typhoon, including contract information showing various Chinese government entities as clients.\n",
  "affiliation_confidence": "high",
  "entity_name": [
    "Sichuan Juxinhe Network Technology Co., Ltd.",
    "Beijing Huanyu Tianqiong Information Technology Co., Ltd.",
    "Sichuan Zhixin Ruijie Network Technology Co., Ltd."
  ],
  "last_seen": "2026",
  "first_seen": "2019",
  "first_described": "2024-09",
  "description": "Salt Typhoon is a Chinese state-sponsored espionage group that executed what the chairman of the Senate Intelligence Committee described as \"the worst telecom hack in our nation's history.\" (Reuters, 2024) The group infiltrated at least nine major U.S. telecommunications companies — including Verizon, AT&T, T-Mobile, — as well as telecoms in over 80 countries, with the campaign running one to two years before discovery in late 2024.\nThe group is believed to have compromised CALEA lawful intercept systems — the infrastructure used by U.S. law enforcement and intelligence agencies for court-authorized surveillance. This gave the group access to lists of individuals under surveillance, intercepted call content and text messages, and metadata. As of May 2026, no detailed technical reporting on the breach. Democratic Senator Maria Cantwell said Verizon and AT&T are blocking release of key documents. (Reuters, 2026)\nThe group targets backbone infrastructure — Cisco routers handling major internet traffic, provider edge and customer edge routers — and modifies them for persistent long-term access. Custom tooling includes the GhostSpider backdoor for persistent access to telecom networks. (Trend Micro, 2024) \nIn May 2026, Bitdefender published a report describing Salt Typhoon/FamousSparrow targeting Azerbaijani oil and gas industry. The campagin, lasting between late December 2025 through late February 2026, exploited Microsoft Exchange server entry point vulnerability to deploy \"two distinct backdoor families, Deed RAT and Terndoor.\"\nOFAC sanctioned Sichuan Juxinhe Network Technology for direct involvement. The August 2025 CISA advisory identified two additional Chinese companies providing services to PLA and MSS units. In November 2025, Australia's ASIO director confirmed Salt Typhoon had targeted Australian telecommunications networks.\n",
